uam's Maxwell scheduler (Mesa 19's nv50_ir, SchedDataCalculatorGM107): the
stall of a block's last instruction when it sets a barrier.

A barrier becomes active a cycle after the instruction that sets it, so an
instruction followed by one waiting on its barrier must stall 2 cycles.
Every block but the first starts by waiting on all six barriers, but
setDelay() looked at the next block's first instruction before visit() had
reached that block: its sched word was still whatever the instruction's
memory held (Instruction::init never set it). The stall was then 2 or not by
chance, so the same shader compiled differently from run to run, and on the
console the next block could read a register its load had not written yet
(the GPU, not emulators, keeps these timings). Now the next block's first
instruction counts as waiting on every barrier, and sched (and serial) start
at 0.

diff --git a/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir.cpp b/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir.cpp
index a7adec0..f616d8d 100644
--- a/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir.cpp
+++ b/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir.cpp
@@ -590,6 +590,9 @@ void Instruction::init()
    predSrc = -1;
    flagsDef = -1;
    flagsSrc = -1;
+
+   serial = 0;
+   sched = 0;
 }
 
 Instruction::Instruction()
diff --git a/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_emit_gm107.cpp b/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_emit_gm107.cpp
index 04717c8..8114d25 100644
--- a/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_emit_gm107.cpp
+++ b/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_emit_gm107.cpp
@@ -4122,7 +4122,10 @@ SchedDataCalculatorGM107::setDelay(Instruction *insn, int delay,
       if (!next) {
          delay = 0x2;
       } else {
-         int wt = getWtDepBar(next);
+         // The first instruction of another block waits on every barrier
+         // (visit() emits that when it reaches the block, so its sched is
+         // not set yet here).
+         int wt = next->bb != insn->bb ? 0x3f : getWtDepBar(next);
          if ((wt & (1 << wr)) | (wt & (1 << rd)))
             delay = 0x2;
       }
